{"authored" [{"question" "q-353904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ about $x=e.$\n\n$x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-e)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-e)^{2}+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ and $a=e.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ f(e) & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)=e \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(e) & =\\frac{1}{e} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(e) & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =e+(2)(x-e)+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{e})}{2!}(x-e)^{2}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})}{3!}(x-e)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =e+2(x-e)+\\frac{1}{2e}(x-e)^{2}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed98c27235f7de1d01c509a5f2" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2e}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x$ about $x=1.$\n\n$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-1)\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-1)^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-4}}\n$(x-1)^{3}+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ f(1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =1+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(1) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x & =1+2(x-1)+\\frac{-1}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{3!}(x-1)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =1+2(x-1)-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{3}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed98c27235f7de1d01c509a5f2" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" 