estion-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=1$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\sqrt{1.2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(1) & ={1}^{1/2}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)+\\frac{(-1/4)}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1.2$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Since\n\n$$\n1.2-1=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1.2} & ≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5})-\\frac{1}{8}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{1}{200} \\\\  & =\\frac{219}{200}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed98c27235f7de1d01c509a5f2" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.095"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $e^{-x}$ centered about the point $x=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/example-752.json" "file_sha256" "d846b6a3647cbc0b9c6e99c3d0c178ef79e23dc01eb271adba4f1b97e9e6ded7" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\frac{f'''(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3+\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=e^{-x}$ and $a=3.$\n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ f(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-{e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(3) & =-{e}^{-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-3}+(-{e}^{-3})(x-3)+\\frac{({e}^{-3})}{2!}(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{(-{e}^{-3})}{3!}(x-3)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & ={e}^{-3}-{e}^{-3}(x-3)+\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{2}(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{6}(x-3)^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/example-752.json" "file_sha256" "d846b6a3647cbc0b9c6e99c3d0c178ef79e23dc01eb271adba4f1b97e9e6ded7" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x) = \\sqrt x$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{9.1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/example-753.json" "file_sha256" "0521b0c38b33d927ec7e4dbd7bec13df611a9d990e01a5228460198bfa630fdb" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x = 9.1$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{9.1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9.1} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(9.1-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(9.1-9)^{2} \\\\  & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(0.1)-\\frac{1}{216}(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.016\\,620,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places. \n\n**Note:** The exact value of $\\sqrt{9.1}$ is $3.016\\,621$ to six decimal places. So, our approximation is very good!" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/example-753.json" "file_sha256" "0521b0c38b33d927ec7e4dbd7bec13df611a9d990e01a5228460198bfa630fdb" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-126846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{x+2}$ about the point $x=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-126846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ and $a=-1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+2}=(x+2)^{-1} \\\\ f(-1) & =(-1+2)^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-(x+2)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-(-1+2)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2(x+2)^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-1) & =2(-1+2)^{-3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-6(x+2)^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-1) & =-6(-1+2)^{-4}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+2} & =1+(-1)(x+1)+\\frac{2}{2!}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{(-6)}{3!}(x+1)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-(x+1)+(x+1)^{2}-(x+1)^{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-126846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6e7db26c98ad76c4a61cac5b702e503c306953ea737723649dd3a34d84b9367"} {"question" "q-126846" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-126846" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{x+2}=1+(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{(x+1)^{4}}{4}+\\frac{(x+1)^{6}}{6}+⋯"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x+2}=1-(x+1)+(x+1)^{2}-(x+1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x+2}=1-(x+1)+\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}+⋯"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x+2}=2+(x+1)+(x+1)^{2}+(x+1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x+2}=2-(x+1)^{2}+(x+1)^{4}-(x+1)^{6}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-126846" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{x+2}=1-(x+1)+(x+1)^{2}-(x+1)^{3}+⋯"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ about $x=e.$\n\n$x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-e)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-e)^{2}+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ and $a=e.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ f(e) & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)=e \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(e) & =\\frac{1}{e} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(e) & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =e+(2)(x-e)+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{e})}{2!}(x-e)^{2}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})}{3!}(x-e)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =e+2(x-e)+\\frac{1}{2e}(x-e)^{2}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6e7db26c98ad76c4a61cac5b702e503c306953ea737723649dd3a34d84b9367"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353904" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2e}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{x+3}$ about the point $x=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}$ and $a=-2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+3}=(x+3)^{-1} \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2+3)^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}=-(x+3)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-2) & =-(-2+3)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x+3)^{3}}=2(x+3)^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =2(-2+3)^{-3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{6}{(x+3)^{4}}=-6(x+3)^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-2) & =-6(-2+3)^{-4}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+3} & =1+(-1)(x+2)+\\frac{2}{2!}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(-6)}{3!}(x+2)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6e7db26c98ad76c4a61cac5b702e503c306953ea737723649dd3a34d84b9367"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1+2(x+2)+3(x+2)^{2}+6(x+2)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{6}+⋯"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{24}+⋯"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-\\frac{(x+2)}{2}+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{24}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-83185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x$ about $x=1.$\n\n$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-1)\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-1)^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-4}}\n$(x-1)^{3}+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ f(1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =1+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(1) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x & =1+2(x-1)+\\frac{-1}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{3!}(x-1)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =1+2(x-1)-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{3}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6e7db26c98ad76c4a61cac5b702e503c306953ea737723649dd3a34d84b9367"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353905" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353748" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ about the point $x=1.$\n\n$\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=${{field-1}}\n$-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-1)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-1)^{2}-⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353748" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}={x}^{-4} \\\\ f(1) & ={1}^{-4}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}=-4{x}^{-5} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-4(1)^{-5}=-4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{20}{{x}^{6}}=20{x}^{-6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =20(1)^{-6}=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}} & =1+(-4)(x-1)+\\frac{20}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(-120)}{3!}(x-1)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-4(x-1)+10(x-1)^{2}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353748" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6e7db26c98ad76c4a61cac5b702e503c306953ea737723649dd3a34d84b9367"} {"question" "q-353748" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353748" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353748" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353748" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353748" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-353748" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=1$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\sqrt{1.2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(1) & ={1}^{1/2}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)+\\frac{(-1/4)}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1.2$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Since\n\n$$\n1.2-1=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1.2} & ≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5})-\\frac{1}{8}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{1}{200} \\\\  & =\\frac{219}{200}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6e7db26c98ad76c4a61cac5b702e503c306953ea737723649dd3a34d84b9367"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-347635" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.095"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-83217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of ${x}^{-1/2}$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of ${8}^{-1/2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-83217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\ f(9) & ={9}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(9)^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-1/2} & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 27}(x-9)+⋯ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{x-9}{54}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=8$ into the above to approximate ${8}^{-1/2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{-1/2} & ≈\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{8-9}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{54}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-83217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6e7db26c98ad76c4a61cac5b702e503c306953ea737723649dd3a34d84b9367"} {"question" "q-83217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-83217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{17}{81}"] ["math" "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{19}{54}"] ["math" "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{5}{12}"] ["math" "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{8}{27}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-83217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{19}{54}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ about $x=0$ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=0.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) \\\\ f(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{0+1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =-\\frac{1}{(0+1)^{2}}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(0) & =\\frac{2}{(0+1)^{3}}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =(0)+(1)(x-0)+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-0)^{2}+\\frac{(2)}{3!}(x-0)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=0.5$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.5 & ≈(0.5)-\\frac{1}{2}(0.5)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(0.5)^{3} \\\\  & =0.417\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6e7db26c98ad76c4a61cac5b702e503c306953ea737723649dd3a34d84b9367"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.412"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.415"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.417"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.421"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.424"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-126875" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5)≈0.417"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-126877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-126877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1+(2)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{(4)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & ≈1+2(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+2(-\\frac{\\pi }{20})+2{(-\\frac{\\pi }{20})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{200} \\\\  & =0.735\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-126877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6e7db26c98ad76c4a61cac5b702e503c306953ea737723649dd3a34d84b9367"} {"question" "q-126877" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-126877" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.732"] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.735"] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.739"] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.744"] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.746"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-126877" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.735"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-9608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{10}.$ Give your answer to $5$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-9608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=10$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{10}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(10-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(10-9)^{2} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{216} \\\\  & =3.162\\,04,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-9608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6e7db26c98ad76c4a61cac5b702e503c306953ea737723649dd3a34d84b9367"} {"question" "q-9608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-9608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{10}≈3.161\\,12"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}≈3.161\\,97"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}≈3.162\\,04"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}≈3.164\\,39"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}≈3.165\\,12"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "q-9608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{10}≈3.162\\,04"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/state.json" "file_sha256" "5baeb0607442a029ceb420151dc247ea8b4e51c7e12c480b13b49b908208b8b9"} {"question" "e-752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $e^{-x}$ centered about the point $x=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/example-752.json" "file_sha256" "aea3e524c8e73aa41f9cb11d887bde864c9eb851725f89c1ad9b5120cb20321e"} {"question" "e-752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\frac{f'''(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3+\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=e^{-x}$ and $a=3.$\n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ f(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-{e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(3) & =-{e}^{-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-3}+(-{e}^{-3})(x-3)+\\frac{({e}^{-3})}{2!}(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{(-{e}^{-3})}{3!}(x-3)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & ={e}^{-3}-{e}^{-3}(x-3)+\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{2}(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{6}(x-3)^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/example-752.json" "file_sha256" "aea3e524c8e73aa41f9cb11d887bde864c9eb851725f89c1ad9b5120cb20321e"} {"question" "e-753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x) = \\sqrt x$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{9.1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/example-753.json" "file_sha256" "4867904099bb9da9842addc043280be2ddaa517e6d2b3460f8fa369f8f35a91a"} {"question" "e-753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x = 9.1$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{9.1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9.1} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(9.1-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(9.1-9)^{2} \\\\  & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(0.1)-\\frac{1}{216}(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.016\\,620,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places. \n\n**Note:** The exact value of $\\sqrt{9.1}$ is $3.016\\,621$ to six decimal places. So, our approximation is very good!" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041885/example-753.json" "file_sha256" "4867904099bb9da9842addc043280be2ddaa517e6d2b3460f8fa369f8f35a91a"}] "usage" {"q-353905" [] "q-9608" [] "q-347635" [] "e-752" [] "e-753" [] "q-353904" []} "basis" 1541 "retractions" [[17592186184826 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nUse the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ about $x = 9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{10}$. Give your answer to $5$ decimal places."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186183846 17592186183847 17592186183848 17592186183849 17592186183850 17592186310616 17592186310619 17592186310621 17592186310623 17592186310626 17592186310628 17592186310630 17592186310632] "immutable_fields" [[17592186183845 :answer-field/correct] [17592186183845 :answer-field/id] [17592186183845 :answer-field/key] [17592186183845 :answer-field/type] [17592186310615 :answer-field/correct] [17592186310615 :answer-field/id] [17592186310615 :answer-field/key] [17592186310615 :answer-field/type] [17592186310618 :answer-field/correct] [17592186310618 :answer-field/id] [17592186310618 :answer-field/key] [17